n governor's race favors Democrat Xavier Becerra over Republican Steve Hilton by a wide margin. The state's D+12 partisan lean, a term-limited incumbent, and a string of September surveys showing Becerra ahead by 18 to 25 points among likely voters all point the same direction. Every major rating outfit calls the race Solid or Safe Democratic, and prediction markets give Becerra roughly a 96 percent chance of winning.\n\nHilton has run an energetic campaign focused on affordability and has slightly outraised Becerra, while headlines question Becerra's low-profile strategy and lagging voter enthusiasm. But polls show Becerra's lead widening rather than shrinking, and his edge has held across pollsters and months. His English-only classroom proposal from Hilton has drawn criticism in a diverse state. Unless something substantial changes in the final weeks, California appears set to extend its Democratic hold on the governor's office.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9963, "margin": 17.38}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.998, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9982, "margin": 20.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.998, "margin": 21.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.9936, "margin": 19.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.9916, "margin": 19.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.996, "margin": 19.0}]}